{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"LANDED (2026-08-07): the two operator-approved remediations, plus R3 after its design answer. Ticket left OPEN — the operator owns its disposition.\n\n## What landed\n\n| | change | what it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| R5 | Gerrit 1507 | a `LockTimeout` NAMES the holder |\n| R1 | Gerrit 1508 | one directory-scoped git walk per compaction instead of one full-history walk per signed event |\n| R3 | Gerrit 1510 | compact-on-close stands aside when the write lock is already busy |\n\nR2, R4 and R6 remain unimplemented (not approved). Per-ticket locking was NOT started — recorded as the future consideration if this round does not deliver.\n\n## R5 — what the timeout now says\n\nScoped by the operator to EXISTING machinery: no v3 stamp, no new fields, no session-id\nresolution, no new parameter on `lock.acquire`. The mkdir leg already wrote\n`rebar-lock v2 host=<host-identity> ns=<pid-ns> pid=<pid> start=<start-time>` and the\ntimeout threw it away. `describe_lock_holder()` now renders it, and `LockTimeout`\nappends it:\n\n    flock: could not acquire lock after 60s\n        (holder: host=name-h pid=4321 start=93117 held=48s pid_state=live)\n\n`pid_state` is the second question a starved writer has — is a process really holding\nthis, or is the stamp stale — and it follows the same refusal-without-proof rule\nreclamation uses (`unprobeable (foreign host)` / `unprobeable (other pid namespace)`),\nso the message can never suggest a reclaim the lock itself would refuse. Every case the\nstamp cannot answer is stated plainly rather than guessed: `unknown (no ownership\nstamp)`, `unknown (unrecognised ownership stamp)` (the documented forward-compat decline\npath), `unknown (incomplete ownership stamp)` (a torn mid-write read). The bash-parity\nmessage prefix is unchanged, and an unreadable stamp degrades to exactly the old string.\n\n## R1 — measured before/after on the real-sized store\n\nMeasured against the read-only 71,041-commit clone (`/tmp/lockbig/.tickets-tracker`),\nresolving the introducing commit for every event of a ticket. OLD = one\n`resolve_event_commit` full-history walk per event; NEW = one\n`build_ticket_position_commit_map` directory-scoped walk for the whole ticket:\n\n| ticket | files | OLD (per-event) | NEW (one walk) | speedup | mismatches |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 030f-c14f-596a-4f79 | 4 | 29.81s | 1.05s | 28x | 0 |\n| df2c-e4b8-496d-4698 | 25 | 193.16s | 4.72s | 41x | 0 |\n| c97f-90ae-4976-47c7 | 73 | 546.00s | 4.27s | 128x | 0 |\n| 5200-e04e-246e-4aae | 155 | **1165.54s** | **2.09s** | **557x** | 0 |\n\n257 events resolved across the four tickets, **0 mismatches** — the batched map returned\na byte-identical commit sha to `resolve_event_commit` for every single one, on the real\n71k-commit history.\n\nThe OLD column is the compaction hold time this ticket measured as the defect: 155 events\nis 1165s (19m26s) of read-only git archaeology holding the store write lock, against a 60s\nacquire budget. It is now 2.09s. Note the NEW column does not grow with the event count —\nit is one directory-scoped walk either way, which is why the 155-file ticket is FASTER than\nthe 25-file one (fewer/shallower trees to walk in its history slice). That is the shape\nchange: the cost stopped being per-signed-event.\n\nThe mechanism is confirmed as diagnosed — it is the pathspec, not the process count. A\nper-file GLOB forces git to evaluate the match at every commit; a bare directory prefix\nlets git prune whole trees. The cost is now per-COMPACTION rather than per-signed-event,\nso it stops scaling with the number of signed events.\n\n## How equivalence was proved\n\nAttribution feeds `verify-authorship`, so equivalence was asserted, not assumed:\n\n1. **Against real history** — the measurement above compares the batched map to\n   `resolve_event_commit` for EVERY event of each ticket on the real 71k-commit store: 0\n   mismatches.\n2. **Against the off-first-parent case explicitly** — a fixture whose history contains an\n   event added on BOTH sides of a merge (the shape a store gets when two clones write the\n   same event and sync). The merge is TREESAME to both parents for that path, so default\n   simplification follows only the first parent and answers with the MAINLINE add, while\n   `--full-history` sees the older side-branch add. The test asserts both resolvers return\n   the side-branch commit AND that the simplified query returns something DIFFERENT — so\n   the fixture provably exercises the case `--full-history` exists for, and cannot silently\n   stop doing so.\n3. **At the call site** — the ledger `_build_authorship_ledger` produces is compared\n   entry-by-entry to the old per-event loop (`resolve_event_commit` then the global\n   `resolve_position_commit` fallback).\n4. **Fail-closed** — a forced empty map (any git failure) is asserted to fall back to the\n   per-event resolver with unchanged shas, so batching can only ever cost time, never a\n   wrong or missing commit.\n\n`--full-history` is kept, the event TYPE stays a non-input (keyed by position), lock\nsemantics/acquisition/scope are untouched, and the ledger stays INSIDE the lock — so R2's\nrace never has to be risked.\n\n## R3 — the skip, and why no floor\n\nApproved after the operator's design answer that the load is INTERMITTENT. Detection uses\nthe existing mechanism: `write_lock_is_busy()` is the same two legs `acquire` takes, in\nthe same order, with a zero deadline. Both legs are probed (an fcntl-only probe would read\na store held by a bash-era or flock-less writer as free), the fcntl leg is released in a\n`finally` whether or not the mkdir leg is taken, a successful mkdir probe removes the dir\nimmediately, and the probe fails OPEN so a probe failure degrades to today's behaviour\nrather than suppressing work on a free store.\n\nSkipping is a no-op for correctness, asserted end-to-end against a real store with a real\nsecond holder: the close still succeeds, the event log is byte-for-byte unchanged, and the\nnext uncontended compaction folds exactly what the skipped one would have (same reduced\nstate). No forced-after-N-skips floor, per the intermittent-load answer; the skip is logged\nat WARNING with the holder so a compaction that never runs again cannot be silent.\n\n## What is NOT closed by this\n\n* R3's floor question stands if the load ever becomes sustained.\n* The acquire-budget shape is untouched: a `LockTimeout` is still terminal and lossy\n  (option 1 of the earlier assessment — durable spool on timeout — remains unimplemented).\n  The measured claim is that R1 removes the incident class, not that loss is now\n  impossible.\n* Post-R1 re-measurement under the 6-writer harness has NOT been rerun; that is the next\n  evidence needed before judging whether per-ticket locking is required."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786122763149061001,"uuid":"59549450-1ae8-4804-b147-36076ec866ea"}